00:04:29.075 --> 00:04:31.200
NATHEN HARVEY: Great, hi,
my name is Nathen Harvey.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:34.170
And I am a developer advocate
here at Google Cloud.

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:38.550
And the area where I focus is
on the DevOps and SRE community.

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:41.130
So what's my
background with DevOps?

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:45.000
I've been sort of circling in
and around the DevOps community

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.770
for, I don't know,
eight or nine years.

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:51.210
And I used to be a DevOp.

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:55.560
I don't believe you can be a
DevOp, but I used to be one.

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:57.130
So what does that mean?

00:04:57.130 --> 00:04:59.940
It means that I used to be
responsible for managing

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:03.030
operations at an
online retailer.

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:07.050
And we would build, deploy,
manage applications.

00:05:07.050 --> 00:05:08.610
I helped manage
the infrastructure.

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:11.730
We were moving from the
data center into the cloud.

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:13.710
And then eventually
I left that job

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:17.200
and became a developer
advocate or community organizer

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:20.250
and have been spending the
last eight years in that space.

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:21.250
BRIAN DORSEY: Excellent.

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:23.110
JEZ HUMBLE: I am also
working in dev rel

00:05:23.110 --> 00:05:24.490
as a developer advocate.

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:28.300
My connection to DevOps
is that I co-authored

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:31.150
a book called "Continuous
Delivery," which came out

00:05:31.150 --> 00:05:34.000
around the same time that the
DevOps movement was really

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:36.270
starting to be a
thing, to the extent

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:38.020
that we actually got
DevOps into the book.

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:39.550
And it was the
first published book

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:42.265
that actually mentions DevOps,
I think, as far as I know.

00:05:42.265 --> 00:05:44.350
I'm also co-author of
the "DevOps Handbook"

00:05:44.350 --> 00:05:47.590
and of "Accelerate," which
is a book based on a research

00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:50.410
program that myself and
Dr. Nicole Forsgren, who's

00:05:50.410 --> 00:05:53.313
also at Google, along with
a bunch of other folks,

00:05:53.313 --> 00:05:55.480
including the people at
Puppet, have been working on

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:57.692
for the last six
years, which looks

00:05:57.692 --> 00:05:59.150
at how to measure
software delivery

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:02.120
performance, the
factors that impact it,

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:04.030
and why it's important.

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:05.850
A whole bunch of
that research is up

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:09.940
at Cloud.Google.com/devops,
along with the last six years

00:06:09.940 --> 00:06:11.485
of state of DevOps reports.

00:06:11.485 --> 00:06:13.360
BRIAN DORSEY: So we're
going to kick this off

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:15.135
with the easy question.

00:06:15.135 --> 00:06:15.760
What is DevOps?

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:17.680
JEZ HUMBLE: Well, the
first thing to say

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:19.930
is that there is no
definition of DevOps.

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:21.370
And that's on purpose.

00:06:21.370 --> 00:06:23.945
The people who really,
I guess we can say

00:06:23.945 --> 00:06:25.570
are the founders of
the movement, which

00:06:25.570 --> 00:06:28.672
is Patrick Debois
and Andrey Schaefer,

00:06:28.672 --> 00:06:30.130
they deliberately
didn't want there

00:06:30.130 --> 00:06:31.380
to be a definition of DevOps.

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:33.088
They didn't want there
to be a manifesto.

00:06:33.088 --> 00:06:34.505
They didn't want
any of that stuff

00:06:34.505 --> 00:06:36.130
because they had seen
what had happened

00:06:36.130 --> 00:06:38.680
to the Agile movement, where
it basically got taken over

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:42.460
by a whole bunch of people
and deployed in order to make

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:45.172
money and sell people stuff
and all those kind of things.

00:06:45.172 --> 00:06:46.630
They're like, we're
not doing that.

00:06:46.630 --> 00:06:48.460
We want this to be I
guess what today we'd

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:50.490
call a community of
practice, basically,

00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:52.330
of people who are
trying to get better

00:06:52.330 --> 00:06:55.510
at building and operating
distributed systems at scale

00:06:55.510 --> 00:06:57.170
that were secure and reliable.

00:06:57.170 --> 00:06:58.410
And that's, when I'm
asked to define it,

00:06:58.410 --> 00:06:59.620
that's how I normally define it.

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:00.790
It's a community
of practice trying

00:07:00.790 --> 00:07:02.290
to solve a particular
problem, which

00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:05.740
is how do we build large
scale distributed systems

00:07:05.740 --> 00:07:07.030
that are secure and resilient?

00:07:07.030 --> 00:07:10.120
However, everyone
wants a definition.

00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:13.120
We have a definition at
Cloud.Google.com/devops.

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:15.550
It's one we made
up by ourselves.

00:07:15.550 --> 00:07:17.718
And it says, "DevOps
is an organization

00:07:17.718 --> 00:07:20.260
and cultural movement that aims
to increase software delivery

00:07:20.260 --> 00:07:23.350
velocity, improve service
reliability, and build share

00:07:23.350 --> 00:07:26.350
ownership among software
stakeholders," which

00:07:26.350 --> 00:07:27.700
is as good as any, I think.

00:07:27.700 --> 00:07:28.540
I don't hate.

00:07:28.540 --> 00:07:29.560
I quite like it.

00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:31.440
Nathen, you must have
thoughts on this.

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:33.940
NATHEN HARVEY: Sure, I have at
least three other definitions

00:07:33.940 --> 00:07:35.620
that I'll run you
through right now.

00:07:35.620 --> 00:07:36.845
That's not actually true.

00:07:36.845 --> 00:07:39.220
I'm not going to run you
through three other definitions.

00:07:39.220 --> 00:07:42.760
But I always like to, when
I'm asked to define DevOps,

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:44.680
I, for better or
worse, just like

00:07:44.680 --> 00:07:46.300
to become a little
bit of a historian

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:50.180
and say, well, how did that
word actually come into being?

00:07:50.180 --> 00:07:54.290
And there are kind of two almost
simultaneous origin stories.

00:07:54.290 --> 00:07:57.100
One is that John Allspaw
and Paul Hammond gave a talk

00:07:57.100 --> 00:07:59.380
at Velocity, and the
talk was titled something

00:07:59.380 --> 00:08:01.060
like 10 Deploys a Day.

00:08:01.060 --> 00:08:03.700
And there was a slide in
that talk that on one side

00:08:03.700 --> 00:08:06.220
had Dev, on the other side
had Ops, and in the middle

00:08:06.220 --> 00:08:07.450
had a heart.

00:08:07.450 --> 00:08:11.170
So that's one part of the
origin story of the term DevOps.

00:08:11.170 --> 00:08:14.770
The other part is that an
Agile conference there was

00:08:14.770 --> 00:08:16.790
a Birds of a Feather session.

00:08:16.790 --> 00:08:20.130
And I think it was Andrew
Clay Schaefer proposed

00:08:20.130 --> 00:08:21.880
a topic at that Birds
of a Feather session

00:08:21.880 --> 00:08:24.017
called Agile infrastructure.

00:08:24.017 --> 00:08:26.350
And then he realized that no
one at the Agile conference

00:08:26.350 --> 00:08:29.740
was going to show up for that,
so he skipped his own Birds

00:08:29.740 --> 00:08:31.120
of a Feather session.

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:33.620
And Patrick Debois did show up.

00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:35.169
So eventually they met.

00:08:35.169 --> 00:08:37.520
And then Patrick
started this whole--

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:40.690
like coined the term DevOps by
starting a conference called

00:08:40.690 --> 00:08:42.320
DevOps Days.

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:44.200
But the interesting
thing at that time,

00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:45.910
I think the reason
that it was called

00:08:45.910 --> 00:08:48.880
DevOps was because
we kind of recognized

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:51.730
this wall of confusion
between dev and ops.

00:08:51.730 --> 00:08:54.010
And the way that I
always talk about it is,

00:08:54.010 --> 00:08:56.650
it's about how we
align those incentives.

00:08:56.650 --> 00:08:58.390
So we tell developers,
your job is

00:08:58.390 --> 00:09:01.600
to build features and
make the customer happy.

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:03.670
And then we turn to
operations and we say,

00:09:03.670 --> 00:09:06.370
your job is to keep
the system stable.

00:09:06.370 --> 00:09:07.600
And so what does that do?

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:10.630
That turns the developers
into push, push, push,

00:09:10.630 --> 00:09:14.010
and the operators into no, stop.

00:09:14.010 --> 00:09:16.510
Because to keep the system
stable, what I will do

00:09:16.510 --> 00:09:19.000
is accept no changes
in the system.

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:21.640
And that became a real problem.

00:09:21.640 --> 00:09:24.400
So DevOps was born
from how do we actually

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:27.820
stop this throwing stuff
over the wall of confusion

00:09:27.820 --> 00:09:32.080
from dev to ops but instead
get dev and ops to realize

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:36.800
that what they're both here to
do is focus on the customer.

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:42.140
And so today, and why DevOps
is this cultural movement,

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:45.220
I really look at it as,
this is how we actually

00:09:45.220 --> 00:09:49.660
get IT to align to the
business and understand,

00:09:49.660 --> 00:09:51.130
what is the business objective?

00:09:51.130 --> 00:09:54.207
And so I think DevOps
is a terrible word.

00:09:54.207 --> 00:09:56.290
JEZ HUMBLE: So one interesting
historical footnote

00:09:56.290 --> 00:09:58.300
to that is we just had
the very last Velocity

00:09:58.300 --> 00:10:00.030
conference in Berlin this year.

00:10:00.030 --> 00:10:01.780
In as much as one book
ended this movement

00:10:01.780 --> 00:10:04.140
was the first Velocity
conference in 2009.

00:10:04.140 --> 00:10:05.140
We just finished it off.

00:10:05.140 --> 00:10:08.260
And of course, that was
co-founded by John Allspaw

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:10.202
and Steve Souders of Google.

00:10:10.202 --> 00:10:11.785
AJA HAMMERLY: Speaking
of conferences,

00:10:11.785 --> 00:10:14.250
the DevOps Days conference
recently celebrated

00:10:14.250 --> 00:10:15.250
its 10-year anniversary.

00:10:15.250 --> 00:10:18.580
And I am ashamed to admit that
I remember the first DevOps

00:10:18.580 --> 00:10:22.010
Days in Seattle happening and
being very confused as to what

00:10:22.010 --> 00:10:23.840
that was.

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:25.700
What's a DevOps Days?

00:10:25.700 --> 00:10:27.810
NATHEN HARVEY: That is
a great question, Aja.

00:10:27.810 --> 00:10:30.000
So a DevOps Days,
as you mentioned,

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:32.030
it's celebrated its
10-year anniversary.

00:10:32.030 --> 00:10:33.530
There's a really
interesting thing--

00:10:33.530 --> 00:10:36.290
I think first we'll just start
with the format of a DevOps

00:10:36.290 --> 00:10:37.220
Days.

00:10:37.220 --> 00:10:40.790
So a DevOps Days is a
locally organized conference,

00:10:40.790 --> 00:10:43.500
which is why there have
been many in Seattle.

00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:46.220
There have been many
in Washington, DC.

00:10:46.220 --> 00:10:48.710
All over the world these
conferences happen.

00:10:48.710 --> 00:10:51.290
But the key behind a
DevOps Days conference

00:10:51.290 --> 00:10:54.590
is really to celebrate
the idea of bringing

00:10:54.590 --> 00:10:56.270
a cross-functional
group of humans

00:10:56.270 --> 00:10:59.270
together to collaborate on
what are the things that

00:10:59.270 --> 00:11:01.970
are top of mind around
how do you do IT better,

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:04.700
how do you DevOp, how do you
align better with the business

00:11:04.700 --> 00:11:06.590
strategy, what are
the cultural changes

00:11:06.590 --> 00:11:09.780
that you need to bring
into your organization?

00:11:09.780 --> 00:11:13.490
And in order to really help
with that collaboration,

00:11:13.490 --> 00:11:16.760
a typical DevOps Days
includes about half

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:21.200
pre-determined content, so talks
from presenters that are maybe

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:23.840
keynotes or 30-minute
talks, and then also

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:26.330
some ignite talks, which
are nice five-minute talks--

00:11:26.330 --> 00:11:28.500
inspire us, but keep it short.

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:30.107
But the other half
of the conference

00:11:30.107 --> 00:11:31.940
is where it gets really,
really interesting.

00:11:31.940 --> 00:11:33.380
The conference
organizers kind of

00:11:33.380 --> 00:11:36.650
turn it over the
conference to the attendees

00:11:36.650 --> 00:11:37.550
of that conference.

00:11:37.550 --> 00:11:41.720
And it's typically done through
a process called open spaces.

00:11:41.720 --> 00:11:45.410
And in open spaces, every
participant at the conference

00:11:45.410 --> 00:11:48.410
is invited to propose
a talk or propose

00:11:48.410 --> 00:11:51.020
a topic, a topic that they
are interested in learning

00:11:51.020 --> 00:11:53.440
more about, something
that they want to share.

00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:55.970
And the only rule is you
have to be passionate enough

00:11:55.970 --> 00:11:59.420
about that topic to
start a conversation.

00:11:59.420 --> 00:12:02.300
From there, the
participants self-organize

00:12:02.300 --> 00:12:04.970
into small discussion
groups and talk

00:12:04.970 --> 00:12:06.870
about each one of those topics.

00:12:06.870 --> 00:12:08.600
And so the idea
behind it is really

00:12:08.600 --> 00:12:11.270
that practitioners, the people
that are out there making

00:12:11.270 --> 00:12:13.460
these changes within
their organizations,

00:12:13.460 --> 00:12:16.520
are sharing with one
another exactly what they're

00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:18.830
doing, where they're running
into friction, how they're

00:12:18.830 --> 00:12:20.540
overcoming that friction.

00:12:20.540 --> 00:12:23.030
And it's a really
great conference series

00:12:23.030 --> 00:12:26.360
to help you connect with
your local community

00:12:26.360 --> 00:12:30.650
and drive the practice of DevOps
and the principles of DevOps

00:12:30.650 --> 00:12:32.790
forward within
your organization.

00:12:32.790 --> 00:12:34.485
AJA HAMMERLY: OK, I
think I get it now.

00:12:34.485 --> 00:12:37.110
You have done a good sales pitch
for the DevOps Days conference

00:12:37.110 --> 00:12:37.808
series.

00:12:37.808 --> 00:12:38.850
NATHEN HARVEY: All right.

00:12:38.850 --> 00:12:41.690
One of the other interesting
things I think about it is it's

00:12:41.690 --> 00:12:43.500
truly locally organized.

00:12:43.500 --> 00:12:46.910
So in 2019, I think
there were 80 DevOps

00:12:46.910 --> 00:12:49.322
Days that happened globally.

00:12:49.322 --> 00:12:50.780
Yeah, I see that
look on your face.

00:12:50.780 --> 00:12:53.270
None of the podcast listeners
can see that look on your face,

00:12:53.270 --> 00:12:54.228
but it is one of shock.

00:12:54.228 --> 00:12:56.433
AJA HAMMERLY: I am making
a giant shock face.

00:12:56.433 --> 00:12:57.350
NATHEN HARVEY: Indeed.

00:12:57.350 --> 00:13:00.260
And the way that
works is each one

00:13:00.260 --> 00:13:03.290
is locally organized by a
local group of volunteers.

00:13:03.290 --> 00:13:06.440
There is a global organizing
committee, of which

00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:07.700
I happen to be a part of.

00:13:07.700 --> 00:13:09.980
But our role as the
global organizers

00:13:09.980 --> 00:13:12.510
is really to provide some
of the infrastructure,

00:13:12.510 --> 00:13:16.490
like the website, some coaching
on what should your agenda look

00:13:16.490 --> 00:13:18.260
like, and some guidance on that.

00:13:18.260 --> 00:13:20.810
But each one is
locally organized,

00:13:20.810 --> 00:13:23.130
operated, and executed.

00:13:23.130 --> 00:13:24.060
BRIAN DORSEY: Sweet.

00:13:24.060 --> 00:13:26.810
OK, since we're mining
things other people said.

00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:30.660
There was this mention
of the Accelerate

00:13:30.660 --> 00:13:32.280
State of DevOps report.

00:13:32.280 --> 00:13:34.700
And that sounds
important and big.

00:13:34.700 --> 00:13:37.350
And you mentioned
research-based.

00:13:37.350 --> 00:13:40.730
So I'm curious,
what is this thing?

00:13:40.730 --> 00:13:42.620
JEZ HUMBLE: Yeah, so
State of DevOps reports

00:13:42.620 --> 00:13:46.640
kind of started off as a
kind of industry report

00:13:46.640 --> 00:13:49.670
produced by Puppet Labs,
as it was back in the day.

00:13:49.670 --> 00:13:53.990
And the very first time, I think
I was involved in year two,

00:13:53.990 --> 00:13:56.875
and when we were
presenting after year two,

00:13:56.875 --> 00:13:58.625
someone walked up to
one of the organizers

00:13:58.625 --> 00:14:02.180
at a Puppet conference
was like, hey, you know

00:14:02.180 --> 00:14:03.820
that you're doing it wrong.

00:14:03.820 --> 00:14:07.700
And the people there were
like, oh, that's interesting.

00:14:07.700 --> 00:14:08.780
And who are you?

00:14:08.780 --> 00:14:11.440
And this person was like,
well, I'm Dr. Nicole Forsgren.

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:14.450
I am a researcher at Utah
State University, a professor

00:14:14.450 --> 00:14:16.190
at Utah State University
who does stuff

00:14:16.190 --> 00:14:17.870
on systems administration.

00:14:17.870 --> 00:14:19.510
I do research on
systems administration

00:14:19.510 --> 00:14:22.730
amongst other things
and I'm a statistician.

00:14:22.730 --> 00:14:24.980
And this person was smart
enough to say, well,

00:14:24.980 --> 00:14:27.230
maybe you should come and
talk to the people who do it

00:14:27.230 --> 00:14:28.290
and be involved in that.

00:14:28.290 --> 00:14:31.520
And so in year
three, 2014, Nicole

00:14:31.520 --> 00:14:35.720
joined us and injected basically
statistical and methodological

00:14:35.720 --> 00:14:36.890
rigor into it.

00:14:36.890 --> 00:14:40.190
And so it ended up being this
research-driven, academically

00:14:40.190 --> 00:14:43.310
rigorous program
looking into how

00:14:43.310 --> 00:14:46.220
you measure software delivery
performance, the impacts

00:14:46.220 --> 00:14:50.390
of that, the things that impact
software delivery performance.

00:14:50.390 --> 00:14:53.450
And so over the last six
years, we've built up,

00:14:53.450 --> 00:14:56.090
I think, more than 30,000
data points of observations

00:14:56.090 --> 00:14:58.520
by this point, from all
different kinds of companies,

00:14:58.520 --> 00:15:00.120
of all different
domains and sizes.

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:01.848
And we have actually
a pretty good idea

00:15:01.848 --> 00:15:03.890
of how you get better at
doing software delivery.

00:15:03.890 --> 00:15:06.590
And we have data and
research that makes

00:15:06.590 --> 00:15:08.573
that useful and applicable.

00:15:08.573 --> 00:15:10.490
AJA HAMMERLY: But what
does the research show?

00:15:10.490 --> 00:15:11.990
JEZ HUMBLE: That's
a great question.

00:15:11.990 --> 00:15:15.140
We actually have
what we lovingly

00:15:15.140 --> 00:15:19.460
refer to as the BFD, or the
Big and Fulsome Diagram,

00:15:19.460 --> 00:15:22.460
which is currently in the
process of being revised.

00:15:22.460 --> 00:15:24.580
And podcast users might
not be able to see this,

00:15:24.580 --> 00:15:25.250
but I'm holding it up.

00:15:25.250 --> 00:15:26.250
AJA HAMMERLY: It's huge.

00:15:26.250 --> 00:15:29.300
JEZ HUMBLE: It's a big piece
of paper with lots of arrows,

00:15:29.300 --> 00:15:31.550
and boxes, and words.

00:15:31.550 --> 00:15:34.273
AJA HAMMERLY: It
looks like scary UML.

00:15:34.273 --> 00:15:34.940
JEZ HUMBLE: Yes.

00:15:34.940 --> 00:15:36.357
And the reason
that I have this is

00:15:36.357 --> 00:15:40.460
because we're revising it to
include the 2019 findings.

00:15:40.460 --> 00:15:43.580
And some poor
person at Google is

00:15:43.580 --> 00:15:45.080
going to be tasked
with turning this

00:15:45.080 --> 00:15:47.270
into something kind of
beautiful and friendly

00:15:47.270 --> 00:15:50.660
that you can print out
and put above your desk,

00:15:50.660 --> 00:15:53.390
or frame and hang above
your bed for that matter

00:15:53.390 --> 00:15:54.890
if that's what does it for you.

00:15:54.890 --> 00:15:56.900
And I mean, broadly
speaking, what

00:15:56.900 --> 00:16:00.285
it does is it shows you what
helps you get better software

00:16:00.285 --> 00:16:01.160
delivery performance.

00:16:01.160 --> 00:16:03.200
And there's
basically four groups

00:16:03.200 --> 00:16:05.990
of things, a whole bunch
of technical practices

00:16:05.990 --> 00:16:08.130
that we lovingly call
continuous delivery,

00:16:08.130 --> 00:16:10.250
obviously because
that was me, so things

00:16:10.250 --> 00:16:13.130
like continuous testing, at
least a couple of architecture,

00:16:13.130 --> 00:16:15.920
building security into the
software delivery process,

00:16:15.920 --> 00:16:17.870
deployment automation
trunk-based development,

00:16:17.870 --> 00:16:19.770
and a bunch of other things.

00:16:19.770 --> 00:16:23.060
Some lean management practices
like managing work in process,

00:16:23.060 --> 00:16:25.280
limiting work in process,
visual management

00:16:25.280 --> 00:16:26.490
feedback from production.

00:16:26.490 --> 00:16:28.130
Some lean product
development practices

00:16:28.130 --> 00:16:30.680
like working in small
batches and making

00:16:30.680 --> 00:16:31.772
the flow work visible.

00:16:31.772 --> 00:16:33.230
And a whole bunch
of cultural stuff

00:16:33.230 --> 00:16:36.710
like, for example,
retrospectives or postmortems,

00:16:36.710 --> 00:16:38.970
creating autonomous
teams and so forth.

00:16:38.970 --> 00:16:41.480
And we show that all those
things impact software delivery

00:16:41.480 --> 00:16:42.380
performance.

00:16:42.380 --> 00:16:45.530
Some of them also lead
to less burned out teams,

00:16:45.530 --> 00:16:47.900
and less deployment
pain, and less rework.

00:16:47.900 --> 00:16:49.840
Some of them increase
job satisfaction.

00:16:49.840 --> 00:16:51.710
Software delivery
performance, in turn,

00:16:51.710 --> 00:16:53.305
increases organizational
performance.

00:16:53.305 --> 00:16:55.430
So we know that getting
better at software delivery

00:16:55.430 --> 00:16:57.620
makes your organization
more effective, as well.

00:16:57.620 --> 00:17:00.233
So it basically says, well, if
you want to get better at this,

00:17:00.233 --> 00:17:02.900
here are all the things that you
need to do to get better at it.

00:17:02.900 --> 00:17:04.040
AJA HAMMERLY: Sounds
like I should read more

00:17:04.040 --> 00:17:05.087
than the first 10 pages.

00:17:05.087 --> 00:17:06.170
JEZ HUMBLE: There's a lot.

00:17:06.170 --> 00:17:10.280
It's definitely a rabbit hole
that you can dive into and get

00:17:10.280 --> 00:17:11.648
very deep into.

00:17:11.648 --> 00:17:13.940
AJA HAMMERLY: I will say that
the parts of it I've read

00:17:13.940 --> 00:17:16.430
have been incredibly
insightful and have

00:17:16.430 --> 00:17:19.135
made me think about
not just the operations

00:17:19.135 --> 00:17:22.040
side, but the software
in general and cultures

00:17:22.040 --> 00:17:24.099
around software teams
in a very different way.

00:17:24.099 --> 00:17:24.849
JEZ HUMBLE: Great.

00:17:24.849 --> 00:17:26.010
Thank you.

00:17:26.010 --> 00:17:28.943
And we've drawn on
Google's research as well.

00:17:28.943 --> 00:17:30.860
And one of the things
that Google's famous for

00:17:30.860 --> 00:17:33.310
is Project Aristotle,
the research

00:17:33.310 --> 00:17:35.310
on how to build high
performing teams at Google.

00:17:35.310 --> 00:17:37.430
We've been able to
replicate that research.

00:17:37.430 --> 00:17:39.380
This year we looked at
code maintainability

00:17:39.380 --> 00:17:42.450
using stuff from Google's
internal programs.

00:17:42.450 --> 00:17:44.690
So a lot of it actually
is replicated stuff

00:17:44.690 --> 00:17:46.095
that has been found in Google.

00:17:46.095 --> 00:17:48.470
But I think what's important
about it is, a lot of people

00:17:48.470 --> 00:17:50.180
say, well, you're Google.

00:17:50.180 --> 00:17:51.560
You're special.

00:17:51.560 --> 00:17:52.640
You can do this stuff.

00:17:52.640 --> 00:17:53.870
We're different.

00:17:53.870 --> 00:17:56.490
And I think what this program
does is say, well, actually,

00:17:56.490 --> 00:17:57.440
you can do this stuff anywhere.

00:17:57.440 --> 00:17:58.898
It's not just
applicable at Google.

00:17:58.898 --> 00:18:00.140
Anyone can get better.

00:18:00.140 --> 00:18:02.150
And of course, the four
of us work at Google.

00:18:02.150 --> 00:18:03.775
And obviously we know
that Google never

00:18:03.775 --> 00:18:07.280
has problems with
developers, pushing features

00:18:07.280 --> 00:18:10.340
as fast as possible,
and being incentivized

00:18:10.340 --> 00:18:12.980
based on building things
that might not necessarily

00:18:12.980 --> 00:18:13.940
be easy to operate.

00:18:13.940 --> 00:18:16.310
So Google's obviously
a perfect unicorn.

00:18:16.310 --> 00:18:18.150
We never have any
internal issues.

00:18:18.150 --> 00:18:20.720
And we are special
and brilliant.

00:18:20.720 --> 00:18:23.300
BRIAN DORSEY: OK, so you've
got all this research.

00:18:23.300 --> 00:18:24.650
It shows some stuff.

00:18:24.650 --> 00:18:26.370
What should people do with that?

00:18:26.370 --> 00:18:27.530
How do you apply this?

00:18:27.530 --> 00:18:29.030
AJA HAMMERLY: I
think you're asking,

00:18:29.030 --> 00:18:30.410
when would I ever use this?

00:18:30.410 --> 00:18:31.880
Or some variation there of.

00:18:31.880 --> 00:18:33.505
BRIAN DORSEY: Or why
bother reading it?

00:18:33.505 --> 00:18:35.190
Or what should I
do with this stuff?

00:18:35.190 --> 00:18:36.565
NATHEN HARVEY:
Yeah, I would love

00:18:36.565 --> 00:18:38.160
to answer that question for you.

00:18:38.160 --> 00:18:40.230
But I'd also love to
answer it not yet.

00:18:40.230 --> 00:18:40.730
[LAUGHTER]

00:18:40.730 --> 00:18:42.653
BRIAN DORSEY: Wait, what?

00:18:42.653 --> 00:18:44.570
AJA HAMMERLY: I don't
think it works that way.

00:18:44.570 --> 00:18:45.858
BRIAN DORSEY: Podcast DevOps.

00:18:45.858 --> 00:18:48.400
AJA HAMMERLY: Are you trying to
get a second podcast episode?

00:18:48.400 --> 00:18:48.700
Is that what you're
trying to do?

00:18:48.700 --> 00:18:50.420
NATHEN HARVEY: No, I don't
want to get a second podcast.

00:18:50.420 --> 00:18:51.230
Maybe I do.

00:18:51.230 --> 00:18:54.140
But I just wanted to
summarize one quick thing

00:18:54.140 --> 00:18:57.020
that the research has
shown us over time.

00:18:57.020 --> 00:18:59.270
So Jez mentioned
that we're Google so

00:18:59.270 --> 00:19:00.980
that probably doesn't apply.

00:19:00.980 --> 00:19:03.470
Or you're from Google, that
probably doesn't apply here.

00:19:03.470 --> 00:19:06.330
Each year the report looks at
a couple of different things.

00:19:06.330 --> 00:19:09.600
But there has been a consistent
thread throughout the years.

00:19:09.600 --> 00:19:12.980
And that is looking at things
like lead time and deployment

00:19:12.980 --> 00:19:15.710
frequency, which if
you'd say the word DevOps

00:19:15.710 --> 00:19:17.960
you might think of
as the dev side.

00:19:17.960 --> 00:19:20.840
And then the change fail rate,
so when you push something

00:19:20.840 --> 00:19:24.590
to production, how often does
it fail or the time to restore.

00:19:24.590 --> 00:19:28.490
And to me both of those feel
more on the operations side.

00:19:28.490 --> 00:19:30.563
And so Jez, the
research shows us

00:19:30.563 --> 00:19:32.480
that we can either do
one or the other, right?

00:19:32.480 --> 00:19:35.630
We can either move fast
or we can be reliable.

00:19:35.630 --> 00:19:36.620
Is that right?

00:19:36.620 --> 00:19:38.570
JEZ HUMBLE: Absolutely
not, Nathen.

00:19:38.570 --> 00:19:40.290
In fact, quite the opposite.

00:19:40.290 --> 00:19:42.350
It shows that high
performers do better

00:19:42.350 --> 00:19:44.160
at both speed and stability.

00:19:44.160 --> 00:19:46.100
It's not in fact a trade-off.

00:19:46.100 --> 00:19:48.940
And every year we
get all the data.

00:19:48.940 --> 00:19:51.260
And we pop it into a--
you can kind of think

00:19:51.260 --> 00:19:55.435
of us as statistical
Harry Potter magic hat

00:19:55.435 --> 00:19:56.435
called cluster analysis.

00:19:56.435 --> 00:19:58.160
And what cluster
analysis does is

00:19:58.160 --> 00:20:01.590
it basically sorts the
participants into groups.

00:20:01.590 --> 00:20:03.950
And we don't tell it how
many groups to create

00:20:03.950 --> 00:20:05.780
or what those groups
should look like.

00:20:05.780 --> 00:20:07.850
But we just say,
here's four variables.

00:20:07.850 --> 00:20:09.450
Make groups out of them.

00:20:09.450 --> 00:20:13.162
And we always end up with
three or four groups.

00:20:13.162 --> 00:20:15.620
And what's interesting is it's
always the case that there's

00:20:15.620 --> 00:20:18.370
one group that does really
well on those four metrics--

00:20:18.370 --> 00:20:19.870
deploy frequency,
lead time, time

00:20:19.870 --> 00:20:21.173
to restore, change fail rate--

00:20:21.173 --> 00:20:23.590
and one group that really does
really badly on those four,

00:20:23.590 --> 00:20:25.840
and then one or two
groups in between that

00:20:25.840 --> 00:20:27.970
are kind of in the
middle and so forth.

00:20:27.970 --> 00:20:29.890
So every year we kind
of hold our breath

00:20:29.890 --> 00:20:32.777
and we're like, what's
going to pop out this year.

00:20:32.777 --> 00:20:34.360
And every year the
same thing pops up,

00:20:34.360 --> 00:20:36.815
which is really cool because
it validates that finding,

00:20:36.815 --> 00:20:39.440
that actually high performers do
better at speed and stability,

00:20:39.440 --> 00:20:42.020
low performers do badly
at speed and stability.

00:20:42.020 --> 00:20:46.710
And the practices that help you
get better, if you do it right,

00:20:46.710 --> 00:20:50.110
help you get faster and more
secure and more available.

00:20:50.110 --> 00:20:52.400
BRIAN DORSEY: OK, so tell
me if I'm wrong here.

00:20:52.400 --> 00:20:54.070
I think the thing
to do actually is

00:20:54.070 --> 00:20:56.500
for people who may
be listening here

00:20:56.500 --> 00:20:58.930
know that thinking more
carefully about some

00:20:58.930 --> 00:21:01.490
of this DevOps stuff is better,
that you can have both speed

00:21:01.490 --> 00:21:01.990
and agility.

00:21:01.990 --> 00:21:04.240
They're trying to convince
other people on their teams

00:21:04.240 --> 00:21:06.430
or their management folks.

00:21:06.430 --> 00:21:09.040
The thing to do is get them to
read this report because it's

00:21:09.040 --> 00:21:11.170
actually really readable
and based on research.

00:21:11.170 --> 00:21:12.130
Maybe that's the thing.

00:21:12.130 --> 00:21:13.140
JEZ HUMBLE: Yes, exactly.

00:21:13.140 --> 00:21:14.710
And it's actually
designed in such a way

00:21:14.710 --> 00:21:15.585
that you can do that.

00:21:15.585 --> 00:21:16.978
So the PDF is quite big.

00:21:16.978 --> 00:21:18.520
I think it's over
80 pages this year.

00:21:18.520 --> 00:21:20.478
But it's designed in such
a way that if there's

00:21:20.478 --> 00:21:23.470
a particular point that you
want to make to your boss,

00:21:23.470 --> 00:21:26.050
there's a couple of pages
that you can just print out

00:21:26.050 --> 00:21:30.530
and lovingly faux
carelessly put on that desk.

00:21:30.530 --> 00:21:32.530
BRIAN DORSEY: This just
happened to end up here.

00:21:32.530 --> 00:21:34.060
JEZ HUMBLE: Yeah, mysterious.

00:21:34.060 --> 00:21:34.810
Here's your tea.

00:21:34.810 --> 00:21:37.180
And here's a little
coaster to put your tea on.

00:21:37.180 --> 00:21:39.320
And oh, look, it's got
these diagrams on it.

00:21:39.320 --> 00:21:42.490
So it's definitely designed to
be able to do that, in as much

00:21:42.490 --> 00:21:43.840
as people are persuaded by data.

00:21:43.840 --> 00:21:45.790
I think in the software
industry we like

00:21:45.790 --> 00:21:47.380
to think we're data-oriented.

00:21:47.380 --> 00:21:48.130
Often we're not.

00:21:48.130 --> 00:21:52.377
We're fashion and
pear-persuaded or influenced.

00:21:52.377 --> 00:21:54.460
But in as much as people
can be influenced by data

00:21:54.460 --> 00:21:55.710
and research, that's the goal.

00:21:55.710 --> 00:21:58.085
NATHEN HARVEY: And so Brian,
coming back to your question

00:21:58.085 --> 00:22:00.670
about how do I actually take
this research and put it

00:22:00.670 --> 00:22:03.580
to work, you know, Jez
mentioned throwing a copy of it

00:22:03.580 --> 00:22:04.540
on your boss's desk.

00:22:04.540 --> 00:22:06.538
That is a great first step.

00:22:06.538 --> 00:22:08.080
But the other things
that you can do,

00:22:08.080 --> 00:22:11.758
if you go to
Cloud.Google.com/DevOps,

00:22:11.758 --> 00:22:14.050
in addition to being able to
download the report there,

00:22:14.050 --> 00:22:17.190
there's also what we call
a DevOps quick check.

00:22:17.190 --> 00:22:19.930
And we will ask you a
series of five questions.

00:22:19.930 --> 00:22:21.820
And then we will
show you where do

00:22:21.820 --> 00:22:24.460
you fit in terms
of your industry

00:22:24.460 --> 00:22:27.040
and in terms of the
research that we've found.

00:22:27.040 --> 00:22:30.330
We will put your organization
as a low performer,

00:22:30.330 --> 00:22:32.380
a medium performer,
a high performer,

00:22:32.380 --> 00:22:33.880
or an elite performer.

00:22:33.880 --> 00:22:38.090
And it is across those four key
metrics that we talked about.

00:22:38.090 --> 00:22:40.180
And once you
understand that, that

00:22:40.180 --> 00:22:43.630
will also give you some insight
onto where you should probably

00:22:43.630 --> 00:22:45.520
focus your effort.

00:22:45.520 --> 00:22:49.090
And so on that same page,
we have a collection

00:22:49.090 --> 00:22:51.520
of key capabilities
that Jez was mentioning,

00:22:51.520 --> 00:22:54.460
around technical capabilities,
cultural capabilities,

00:22:54.460 --> 00:22:55.580
and so forth.

00:22:55.580 --> 00:22:58.510
So really the idea there is
that you focus your resources

00:22:58.510 --> 00:23:00.730
on what's currently
holding you back,

00:23:00.730 --> 00:23:03.820
identify some constraints,
choose a target, and iterate.

00:23:03.820 --> 00:23:05.440
Make those a little bit better.

00:23:05.440 --> 00:23:08.230
Get a little bit better
around this capability.

00:23:08.230 --> 00:23:09.730
And then reflect.

00:23:09.730 --> 00:23:11.300
And do that same thing again.

00:23:11.300 --> 00:23:13.910
So this is an iterative process.

00:23:13.910 --> 00:23:16.990
The truth is that when you start
your DevOps "transformation"--

00:23:16.990 --> 00:23:18.660
and I put that in air quotes--

00:23:18.660 --> 00:23:19.630
you're never done.

00:23:19.630 --> 00:23:20.510
It is a journey.

00:23:20.510 --> 00:23:23.440
You don't wake up one day and
say, well, now we're DevOps.

00:23:23.440 --> 00:23:24.967
So what's next?

00:23:24.967 --> 00:23:27.300
AJA HAMMERLY: So it's like
everything else in software--

00:23:27.300 --> 00:23:29.840
shipping it is not actually
the interesting part.

00:23:29.840 --> 00:23:33.110
It's how you keep it going, and
improve it, and iterate on it,

00:23:33.110 --> 00:23:34.910
and make it meet the
needs of the humans.

00:23:34.910 --> 00:23:37.840
You have to use it
as life goes on.

00:23:37.840 --> 00:23:38.930
That's the cool part.

