00:28:01.130 --> 00:28:02.750
because I don't know the answer.

00:28:02.750 --> 00:28:04.730
And I don't have to play dumb.

00:28:04.730 --> 00:28:06.500
I'm actually dumb.

00:28:06.500 --> 00:28:09.390
Wrong way to say that, but
I don't know the answer.

00:28:09.390 --> 00:28:12.338
So there's this thing
called the Cloud SQL proxy

00:28:12.338 --> 00:28:13.880
that it seems like
you need, whenever

00:28:13.880 --> 00:28:15.670
you want to talk to Cloud SQL.

00:28:15.670 --> 00:28:16.310
Why?

00:28:16.310 --> 00:28:17.060
Where does it sit?

00:28:17.060 --> 00:28:18.018
What is it for?

00:28:18.018 --> 00:28:18.810
I don't understand.

00:28:18.810 --> 00:28:19.790
AJA HAMMERLY: So the
first thing to know

00:28:19.790 --> 00:28:20.960
is that you don't need it.

00:28:20.960 --> 00:28:23.810
It is just the most recommended
way of accessing Cloud SQL.

00:28:23.810 --> 00:28:25.680
And to kind of give
an answer to this,

00:28:25.680 --> 00:28:28.530
I'm going to use a little
bit of my history in tech.

00:28:28.530 --> 00:28:30.380
So back in the day
before I was at Google--

00:28:30.380 --> 00:28:32.463
actually before I was even
at the job before I was

00:28:32.463 --> 00:28:33.467
at Google--

00:28:33.467 --> 00:28:35.300
whenever we needed to
configure our database

00:28:35.300 --> 00:28:36.920
to talk to our
application servers

00:28:36.920 --> 00:28:39.260
we had to give our
application servers

00:28:39.260 --> 00:28:41.480
the IP address of the database.

00:28:41.480 --> 00:28:43.190
And then we had to
allow the database

00:28:43.190 --> 00:28:45.800
to access an IP
address in the range

00:28:45.800 --> 00:28:48.780
that we knew our application
servers were going to live in.

00:28:48.780 --> 00:28:51.620
And this is fine.

00:28:51.620 --> 00:28:53.510
But it is not the most
secure, because it

00:28:53.510 --> 00:28:55.400
means that if your
database moves

00:28:55.400 --> 00:28:56.900
or if someone starts
spoofing an IP

00:28:56.900 --> 00:28:59.000
address or all sorts
of other awful things

00:28:59.000 --> 00:29:01.928
that can happen on the internet,
someone could theoretically

00:29:01.928 --> 00:29:04.220
break into your database if
they also have the database

00:29:04.220 --> 00:29:07.280
password and credentials.

00:29:07.280 --> 00:29:08.310
It is much better.

00:29:08.310 --> 00:29:10.220
And also it's just not
particularly flexible

00:29:10.220 --> 00:29:12.428
because it meant that if we
moved the database around

00:29:12.428 --> 00:29:15.450
or if we added servers we
had to redeploy everything.

00:29:15.450 --> 00:29:16.910
So what the Cloud
SQL proxy does is

00:29:16.910 --> 00:29:19.160
it makes it much easier to
interact with your database

00:29:19.160 --> 00:29:20.120
in a secure way.

00:29:20.120 --> 00:29:21.545
There is a proxy
application that

00:29:21.545 --> 00:29:23.420
either runs on your
local development machine

00:29:23.420 --> 00:29:24.320
if you want to do that.

00:29:24.320 --> 00:29:25.778
I can't say that
I highly recommend

00:29:25.778 --> 00:29:29.245
that for a lot of pre-prod
scenarios like staging

00:29:29.245 --> 00:29:30.620
or even development
environments.

00:29:30.620 --> 00:29:33.573
That's not a horrible
way to do things.

00:29:33.573 --> 00:29:35.490
Or it can run on like
your App Engine cluster.

00:29:35.490 --> 00:29:38.030
It's really easy to enable
an App Engine in the app.yaml

00:29:38.030 --> 00:29:42.860
or in some of your other compute
variations, GCE, potentially

00:29:42.860 --> 00:29:44.117
Cloud Run and others.

00:29:44.117 --> 00:29:45.950
And there's just a
little application there.

00:29:45.950 --> 00:29:49.100
And all it does is it listens
to all of your database commands

00:29:49.100 --> 00:29:51.260
and sends them up to the
other side of the proxy,

00:29:51.260 --> 00:29:53.600
which lives on GCP.

00:29:53.600 --> 00:29:56.460
It validates that your
credentials are correct.

00:29:56.460 --> 00:29:58.410
These are your IAM credentials.

00:29:58.410 --> 00:29:59.570
There's a couple different
ways to use them.

00:29:59.570 --> 00:30:00.945
But these are the
IAM credentials

00:30:00.945 --> 00:30:04.850
that you can control through
either the IAM commands G Cloud

00:30:04.850 --> 00:30:07.820
command line or also just
through the Cloud Console.

00:30:07.820 --> 00:30:10.070
And then it forwards
them onto the database

00:30:10.070 --> 00:30:12.410
so that you don't
actually have to connect

00:30:12.410 --> 00:30:15.135
to your database via
IP or your servers

00:30:15.135 --> 00:30:17.490
to your database via IP.

00:30:17.490 --> 00:30:20.400
And so it basically adds
additional layers of security

00:30:20.400 --> 00:30:23.490
and also flexibility by
adding a layer of indirection

00:30:23.490 --> 00:30:26.670
between your application
and your database, which

00:30:26.670 --> 00:30:28.790
just works better for a
cloud environment, where

00:30:28.790 --> 00:30:29.610
things move around.

00:30:29.610 --> 00:30:31.800
You might need to
connect applications.

00:30:31.800 --> 00:30:34.920
And it also works better because
you can control the access

00:30:34.920 --> 00:30:36.600
control in a much
more fine-grained way

00:30:36.600 --> 00:30:39.030
because you can know who,
based on the credentials,

00:30:39.030 --> 00:30:40.960
not just what IP accessed it.

00:30:40.960 --> 00:30:42.570
And I think that is correct.

00:30:42.570 --> 00:30:44.940
But there will be a
link in the show notes

00:30:44.940 --> 00:30:46.920
so that all you all
can go read the blog

00:30:46.920 --> 00:30:49.260
posts or the documentation
page and tell me

00:30:49.260 --> 00:30:50.470
the bits I got wrong.

00:30:50.470 --> 00:30:52.470
BRIAN DORSEY: Ooh, that
sounds like a challenge.

00:30:52.470 --> 00:30:54.540
AJA HAMMERLY: I like
learning things too.

00:30:54.540 --> 00:30:56.998
BRIAN DORSEY: So let me see if
I understood this correctly.

00:30:56.998 --> 00:30:59.400
So the common production
case is that you

00:30:59.400 --> 00:31:03.420
have a copy of this proxy
running on every machine VM

00:31:03.420 --> 00:31:04.500
environment.

00:31:04.500 --> 00:31:07.440
And the stuff there doesn't
need to know about passwords

00:31:07.440 --> 00:31:08.250
or IP addresses.

00:31:08.250 --> 00:31:10.170
It just talks to
the local proxy.

00:31:10.170 --> 00:31:12.480
And the proxy does authen
in an integrated cloud way.

00:31:12.480 --> 00:31:14.670
AJA HAMMERLY: It does need
to know about passwords.

00:31:14.670 --> 00:31:16.410
Because every time you're
accessing a database

00:31:16.410 --> 00:31:18.327
you have to both
authenticate into the machine

00:31:18.327 --> 00:31:20.202
and then also authenticate
into the database.

00:31:20.202 --> 00:31:22.440
And this takes care of
authenticating to the machine.

00:31:22.440 --> 00:31:23.010
BRIAN DORSEY: Got it.

00:31:23.010 --> 00:31:23.850
AJA HAMMERLY:
Basically verifying

00:31:23.850 --> 00:31:25.808
who can access the
machine-- but you still need

00:31:25.808 --> 00:31:27.930
to have your valid
password or account

00:31:27.930 --> 00:31:31.500
to access the database because
your database account controls

00:31:31.500 --> 00:31:34.030
your permissions for the
data in the database.

00:31:34.030 --> 00:31:36.870
So databases contain very
important information.

00:31:36.870 --> 00:31:38.370
And we should secure
them carefully.

00:31:38.370 --> 00:31:39.450
BRIAN DORSEY: No kidding.

00:31:39.450 --> 00:31:40.840
Somebody is going
to disagree with me,

00:31:40.840 --> 00:31:42.882
but this sounds a little
bit like two-factor auth

00:31:42.882 --> 00:31:45.480
for a database
connection in a way.

00:31:45.480 --> 00:31:46.060
Cool.

